{"kind":"event","event_id":6967,"event_uid":"01M2NMR154GZ3HC73QF2EJ4RTR","origin_instance_uid":"01M29GPTT4YE7ZYSCW8FVMKZ1J","type":"issue.commented","project_id":31,"project_uid":"01M2HBYF26YQWA0S07E1J3NSGF","project_name":"popmechanic-ultrapowers","issue_id":298,"issue_uid":"01M2NMBKZXQ77J2J33T01SM5JM","issue_short_id":"m5jm","actor":"exam:1@run-163","hlc_physical_ms":1789580280996,"hlc_counter":0,"content_hash":"4ac69af60ba741fad4323d39fd316eb3f7d4d478b70f9a0609e4fa70bf6a0dbd","payload":{"comment_uid":"01M2NMR152R0YA6SHE7S0D6N23","author":"exam:1@run-163","body":"exam written (examiner, run-163 task 1).\n\nWhat I left in `fleet/tests/test_run_engine_re_edge.mjs`:\n\n- **(d.5) first half — leg (a) [M1]**: A and B, no plan edge, width 2, `foldAgeMs: 0` through the new defaulted `extra` knob on `driveHub`. A's implementer holds until `worker:start impl:B` is on the log — so B's dispatch, and the head it went out on, is READ before A lands — then writes its file and returns done; B's first implementer holds until a `driver:wave-adopted` naming A is on the log, files `blocks` A -> B through the fake hub and returns BLOCKED. Asserted: exactly one `driver:re-edged` with `task` 'B' and `blockedBy` `['A']`, no `fix:B:` start and no `review:B:` start before that event, two `worker:start impl:B` rows with the second after both the adoption and the re-edge and its `head` equal to that adoption's `headSha`, B's row `done`, `unfinished` `[]`, `coverage.complete` true, adoptions naming both A and B. The A-side hold is rig, not a substituted action: without it the scenario is a race between two lanes and the leg is about the ordering.\n- **(d.5) second half — leg (b) [M2]**: the same two tasks with the plan edge A -> B, so B's one dispatch goes out on a head that carries A (asserted: its `worker:start` head equals the adoption's `headSha`); B files the same link and returns BLOCKED. Asserted: no `driver:re-edged`, one `impl:B` start, row `failed` with `reviewVerdict` `not-reviewed`.\n- **leg (d) [M3]**: the second and third `Run:` re-read in the exam — `not yet adopted` nowhere in `fleet/CONTRACT.md`, and the `The re-edge (#979)` -> `dispatched again` range, flattened the way `tr '\\n' ' '` flattens it, carrying `not adopted in the head`.\n- **leg (e)**: the fourth `Run:` re-read over this file itself — no line matching `^import .*exams/`.\n- **leg (c)**: (d.1)-(d.4) are untouched. `driveHub` gained `edges = []` and `extra = {}`, both defaulting to exactly what those four already passed. The header's M4 restatement now reads 'not adopted in the head the dispatch went out on'; (d.1)'s own assertion message still says 'not yet adopted' — prose the task explicitly leaves alone, and the `Run:` that forbids the phrase reads CONTRACT.md only.\n\nPlacement: the new static legs (d)/(e) run at the FOOT of the file, after (d.5), so the first thing that fails at BASE is the scheduling gap rather than a sentence in a document.\n\nVerified: red at BASE on leg (a)'s `driver:re-edged` assertion (0 !== 1, log shows `impl:B` opened, A adopted, B blocked, no re-edge) with every sim precondition passing; green (3/3 runs) under a scratch implementation that snapshots `new Set(adoptedIds)` beside the `const head = adoptedHead` read in `dispatchOnce` and `retryParkedInfra` and has `blockingSiblingsOf` read that snapshot, plus the CONTRACT sentence — scratch patch reverted, only the exam file is left modified. All four `Run:` lines pass under that patch; `test_sims_are_hermetic.mjs` sweeps the file clean.\n\nOne thing the exam does NOT catch, worth naming for the reviewer: a snapshot taken AFTER `await anchorClone` still passes leg (a), because B's dispatch in this rig anchors at BASE (no git work) and A is only adopted once B's worker has opened. The run-161 placement finding is not observable through the engine's seams in a scenario leg (a) can be written from; it stays the implementer's and the reviewer's to hold.","created_at":"2026-09-16T17:38:00.994366123Z"},"created_at":"2026-09-16T17:38:00.996Z"}
